<h1>Backfill historical data</h1>
<p>When connecting a new destination or onboarding a new dataset, you often need to ingest historical records in addition to streaming new changes. This recipe demonstrates how to run large-scale historical backfills through a <a href="/concepts-pipelines">pipeline</a> safely without exhausting rate limits or blocking live <a href="/concepts-events">events</a>.</p>

<div class='alert alert--info'>
  <div class='alert__title'>Prerequisites</div>
  <div class='alert__body'><p>Ensure your source and destination <a href="/concepts-connectors">connectors</a> are active and verified. See <a href="/guides/connecting-a-data-source">Connecting a data source</a> for setup details.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Architecture overview</h2>
<p>Rather than modifying your live streaming pipeline, create a dedicated backfill pipeline with bounded source offsets. This provides two key benefits:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Workload isolation:</strong> High-throughput backfill traffic does not starve or add latency to real-time events.</li>
